---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import RelatedPages from '../components/RelatedPages.astro';
import { articleSchema } from '../lib/schema';
import Fact from '../components/Fact.astro';
import GuideSources from '../components/GuideSources.astro';
import NameCheckForm from '../components/NameCheckForm.astro';
import { normalizeCompanyName } from '../lib/company-name/normalize';

// Worked examples, computed with the same code the checker uses.
const examples = [
  ['Acme Widgets Ltd', 'Acme Widgets Limited'],
  ['The Bakery Ltd', 'Bakery Ltd'],
  ['Smith & Sons Ltd', 'Smith and Son Ltd'],
  ['Back 2 Basics Ltd', 'Back To Basics Ltd'],
  ['Acme (UK) Ltd', 'Acme.com Ltd'],
  ['Café Nero Ltd', 'Cafe Nero Ltd'],
  ['A.B.C. Services Ltd', 'ABC Service Ltd'],
  ['Acme Trading Ltd', 'Acme Ltd'],
].map(([a, b]) => ({ a, b, keyA: normalizeCompanyName(a), keyB: normalizeCompanyName(b) }));
---

<Base
  title="UK company name rules: the 'same as' test explained"
  description="How Companies House decides whether two company names are the 'same as' each other, with the rules from the 2015 Names Regulations and worked examples."
  ogType="article"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Guides', href: '/guides/' }, { name: 'Company name rules', href: '/company-name-rules/' }]}
  schema={[articleSchema({ title: "UK company name rules: the 'same as' test explained", description: "How Companies House decides whether two company names are the 'same as' each other, with the rules from the 2015 Names Regulations and worked examples.", href: '/company-name-rules/', updated: '2026-10-31' })]}
>
  <h1>UK company name rules: the "same as" test</h1>
  <p>
    You can't register a company with a name that is the "same as" one already on the Companies House register. "Same"
    doesn't mean identical. Before comparing, Companies House strips away the parts of a name that don't make it
    different. Those rules are set out in Schedule 3 of the 2015 Names Regulations.
  </p>

  <h2>What gets ignored, in order</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Accents.</strong> "Café" counts as "Cafe", and "Æ" as "AE".</li>
    <li><strong>The company type at the end.</strong> "Limited", "Ltd", "PLC", "LLP", "CIC" and their Welsh versions, with or without full stops.</li>
    <li><strong>Equivalent words and symbols</strong> (as separate words): "&amp;" and "and", "+" and "plus", "2", "two", "to" and "too", "4", "four" and "for", other numbers and their words, "£" and "pound", "%" and "per cent", "@" and "at".</li>
    <li><strong>Endings like "Company", "Co", "UK", ".com", "GB", "Wales"</strong> at the end of the name, alone or combined, including in brackets, as in "(UK)".</li>
    <li><strong>Punctuation</strong> such as full stops, commas, hyphens, apostrophes, brackets and quotes, and the symbols *, = and #.</li>
    <li><strong>A final "S".</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Everything after the first <Fact id="name-same-as-compared-length" /></strong>, counting spaces.</li>
    <li><strong>"The", "www" or "@" at the start.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Spaces.</strong></li>
  </ol>
  <p>If what's left is identical for two names, they count as the same.</p>

  <h2>Examples</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Name</th>
          <th scope="col">Compared as</th>
          <th scope="col">Name</th>
          <th scope="col">Compared as</th>
          <th scope="col">Same?</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          examples.map((e) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{e.a}</td>
              <td>
                <code>{e.keyA}</code>
              </td>
              <td>{e.b}</td>
              <td>
                <code>{e.keyB}</code>
              </td>
              <td>{e.keyA === e.keyB ? 'Yes' : 'No'}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Other rules that can still block a name</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>"Too like" names.</strong> Even if a name isn't the same, an existing company can object if yours is too
      similar to theirs. Our checker lists similar registered names so you can judge.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sensitive words.</strong> Some words, such as ones suggesting a connection with government or a regulated
      profession, need permission before you can use them. Companies House publishes the list in its
      <a href="https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/incorporation-and-names" rel="nofollow noopener">incorporation and names guidance</a>.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Length.</strong> A company name can be at most <Fact id="name-max-length" />.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Trade marks.</strong> Companies House doesn't check trade marks. Search the
      <a href="https://www.gov.uk/search-for-trademark" rel="nofollow noopener">UK trade marks register</a> separately.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    There is one exception to the "same as" rule: a company in the same group can register a same-as name with the
    existing company's consent.
  </p>

  <h2>Check your name</h2>
  <NameCheckForm />

  <RelatedPages hrefs={['/', '/business-name-generator/', '/change-company-name/', '/trade-mark-search/']} />

  <GuideSources
    factIds={['name-same-as-compared-length', 'name-max-length']}
    extra={[
      { title: 'Incorporation and names (Companies House guidance)', url: 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/incorporation-and-names' },
    ]}
  />
</Base>

<style>
  .table-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
  code {
    font-size: 0.9em;
    word-break: break-all;
  }
  li {
    margin: 0.35rem 0;
  }
</style>
